<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bharhut</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bharhut is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satna-district">Satna district</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, site of a Buddhist stupa of the 2nd century BCE. Its carved stone railings and gateways, decorated with scenes from the life of the Buddha and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> and with labelled inscriptions, are among the earliest masterpieces of Indian art. Most surviving pieces are now in the Indian Museum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bharhut.jpg" alt="Bharhut" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cunningham, Alexander, Sir, 1814-1893 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bharhut_stupa_original_layout.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
